![The Lithgow zig zag map](cover.jpg)

Image: [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lithgow_zig_zag_map.jpg)

An allocator in zig lets us dynamically create the memory spaces
for the data types.

```zig
var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
const allocator = gpa.allocator();
```

The data type for a string is the following in _zig_.

```zig
[]const u8
```

For example, when creating a struct

```zig
const Persona = struct {
    nombre: []const u8 = "",
    apellido: []const u8 = "",
};
```

Now we can create a map. There are two types that will mainly be used:
`AutoHashMap` and `StringHashMap`.

- `AutoHashMap`: Lets you create a map with a `key` and `value` of any type.
- `StringHashMap`: Lets you create a map with a string `key` and a `value` of any type.

```zig
var personas = std.AutoHashMap(u8, Persona).init(allocator);
defer personas.deinit();

// Llenamos el mapa
try personas.put(1, Persona{.nombre = "Camilo", .apellido = "Castro"});

// Mapa de Mapas
// Notar que usamos el mismo tipo que usamos en el mapa de personas
var pais = std.StringHashMap(std.AutoHashMap(u8, Persona)).init(allocator);
defer pais.deinit();

try pais.put("chile", personas);

// Obtenemos el valor
const habitantes = pais.get("chile").?;
```

Note: `try` and `defer` can only be used inside functions;
if used in the global scope they will throw an error similar to

```text
src/main.zig:7:50: error: expected type expression,
found 'try' var h = std.AutoHashMap(u8, i32).init(allocator);
```

This is because if an error occurs in `try` it cannot be caught
when used in the global scope.

With the complete code it would look similar to the following:

```zig
var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
const allocator = gpa.allocator();

const Persona = struct {
    nombre: []const u8 = "",
    apellido: []const u8 = "",
};

pub fn main() !void {
    var personas = std.AutoHashMap(u8, Persona).init(allocator);
    defer personas.deinit();

    // Llenamos el mapa
    try personas.put(1, Persona{.nombre = "Camilo", .apellido = "Castro"});

    // Mapa de Mapas
    var pais = std.StringHashMap(std.AutoHashMap(u8, Persona)).init(allocator);
    defer pais.deinit();

    try pais.put("chile", personas);

    // Obtenemos el valor
    const habitantes = pais.get("chile").?;
    const camilo = habitantes.get(1).?;

    // Mostramos en stderr
    std.debug.print("Hola {s} {s}.\n", .{camilo.nombre, camilo.apellido});
}
```

Some curious characteristics of zig: it also has no pattern matching,
because generating the associated low-level code would not be very deterministic.
In this case it is simply recommended to use `if`.

Another curiosity: when compiling _C_ code and mixing it with _Zig_,
zig's internal mapping cannot handle _C_ macros. Keep that
in mind if you want to use a _C_ library in _Zig_.
